; Defending the 84. Winning 42 pp. 69-73 (S-0095-a, S-0096-a, S-0097-c,
; S-0098-a, S-0098-c, S-0099-a, S-0099-b, S-0100-a) and p. 82 (S-0111-f, no
; trump); played in Hands 34 and 35 (S-0147-b, S-0147-c, S-0148-a, S-0148-c,
; S-0148-f, S-0150-h, S-0151-d, S-0151-f).
; Rule, in our words: against a bid of every trick the bidder saves one off
; for the last trick, so a setter keeps what could take it: a double while a
; tile that leads its suit is still out (its watch list), and a pair, two
; tiles of a suit whose double is out, the higher above a tile that leads the
; suit (he follows the double with the lower and takes the off with the
; higher). When he cannot follow he throws, in order: a tile that can take
; nothing and guards no pair; then a tile that keeps a pair tile from being
; forced by the double of its other suit; then a pair tile. A double with
; tiles still out goes last: doubles before pairs, always (p. 72). At no trump
; he defends the same way and keeps his count too, which a points bid needs
; for the set (p. 82).
; Reading: "watched-by", "pair-target", "keeps-a-pair", "protects-a-pair" and
; "wins-nothing" in the prelude; watch-list.silas and pair-targets.silas ask
; them as queries. A throw on the bidder's trump lead is the same throw.
; GAP aggregate: between two pairs, or two doubles, the book keeps the suit
; with more tiles out (S-0092-a, S-0097-a, S-0100-a); this file allows either.
; GAP likelihood: the book throws first a low tile that tells the partner
; which double he may drop (S-0100-b), and the bidder's partner keeps low
; tiles as decoys (S-0092-b, S-0147-a); neither is encoded.
; The book against itself: in Hand 35 trick 4 the left setter says nothing
; has changed and throws the 5-0, breaking the pair of fives that still
; catches the 5-2 or the 5-1, and keeps the 3-0 of a pair of treys whose last
; target, the 3-2, the partner threw on trick 3 (S-0151-b). This file throws
; the 3-0 there. Re-read 2026-10-14 (#153), presuming the encoding misread:
; the book's own trick 3 has the left setter note "another trey out" (the
; 3-2) and throw the 6-0, and the partner then throw the 3-2 after him; at
; trick 4 the pair targets from his chair are exactly the 5-1 and the 5-2
; (pair-targets.silas). The premise "nothing has changed" is false on the
; book's own record, not on a condition the header omits; the row stays as
; filed. In the deal as dealt neither pair catches the bidder's off, the 6-3. And Hand 34's lesson (S-0149-b) says the left setter might
; have kept the fours over his 3-3 with one trey out to two fours; its own
; rule (p. 72) is doubles over pairs, and on the narrated (legal) order one
; trey is out to two fours (the archive's adjudication B1). This file keeps
; the double, as the hand does (S-0148-f).
; New: no gate has it. The dead double (dead-double.silas) is its first case:
; a double whose watch list is empty wins nothing.

(include book)

(define defending-the-last-trick (roles)
  (some (roles me) (viewer me) (setter me))
  (or (every-trick) (none (roles t) (trump t))))

(policy defend-the-84
  (rule throw-what-wins-nothing (play t)
    (defending-the-last-trick)
    (discard t)
    (wins-nothing t)
    (not (protects-a-pair t)))
  (rule then-a-protector (play t)
    (defending-the-last-trick)
    (discard t)
    (wins-nothing t))
  (rule a-pair-before-a-double (play t)
    (defending-the-last-trick)
    (discard t)
    (not (trump-tile t))
    (or (every-trick) (count t 0))
    (none (roles x) (watched-by t x))))
